// ==============================================================================
// FILE: selfhost/gate/ledger/seams.pilot
// THE SEAM REGISTRY: every intentionally unconnected entry, exit or value, and
// every stand-in representation.
// ==============================================================================
// A seam marks a place where code stops on purpose because what it connects to
// does not exist yet. In source it is a call to the built-in `seam`, given the
// seam's id and a short note, as the whole body of an empty entry point or at
// the hand-off of an exit point. Reaching one at run time always fails with
// `SEAM001` and the seam's id, so a seam can never pass for a working feature.
//
// Seams replace placeholder values: never return a constant, a fake pointer or
// a value chosen to satisfy a test where real behaviour is missing. Leave the
// code broken at a seam, test the parts on each side of it on their own, and
// list the seam here.
//
// `pilot gate` (the `seams` gate) keeps this registry and the source in step:
// every `seam` call in `stdlib/` and `selfhost/` must have exactly one entry
// here, and every entry must still have a call. Connecting a seam means
// replacing its call with the real code and deleting its entry in the same
// change.
//
// Two data-side kinds sit beside the call seams (see `stdlib/core/pending.pilot`):
//   * a pending value, `pending("<id>", "<note>")` typed `Pending<T>`, holds no
//     `T` yet and fails with SEAM001 when read. Registered here with kind
//     `::Value`.
//   * a stand-in, `[StandIn("<id>", "<real>", "<how it differs>", "<owner>")]`,
//     is working code with a temporary representation. Registered in
//     `stand_in_registry()`, and named in the `blocked_by` of every wiring
//     ledger row it affects; those rows stay at DRAFT or below until it goes.
pub pkg Pilot.SelfHost.Gate.Ledger.Seams;

/// Which side of the missing piece a seam stands on.
pub enum SeamKind : u8 {
    /// Something calls in here, and the body that should answer is missing.
    Entry,
    /// The code here is done and must hand its result to something missing.
    Exit,
    /// A `Pending<T>` value: data whose real type cannot be represented yet.
    Value,
}

/// One registered seam.
pub struct SeamEntry : IValue {
    /// The id passed to `seam(..)`: `<area>.<what>`, for example
    /// `ffi.load_library`.
    pub id: str;
    pub kind: SeamKind;
    /// What the seam will connect to once it exists.
    pub connects_to: str;
    /// The milestone that connects it (`F6`, `M17`, ..).
    pub owner: str;
}

fn seam_entry(id: str, kind: SeamKind, connects_to: str, owner: str) -> SeamEntry {
    return SeamEntry { id: id, kind: kind, connects_to: connects_to, owner: owner };
}

